meta {
  name: Create Quote Recap (Replay)
  type: http
  seq: 5
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/quote-recaps
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "leadId": "{{createdLeadId}}",
    "policies": [
      { "policyType": "Auto", "premium": 1200.10, "itemCount": 2 },
      {
        "policyType": "Home",
        "premium": 899.95,
        "itemCount": 1,
        "sameAsHousehold": true
      }
    ],
    "insuranceRenewalMonth": "March",
    "notes": "Bundled auto + home. Client wants to review the deductible.",
    "quoteDocument": {
      "key": "{{quoteDocumentKey}}",
      "filename": "carrier-quote.pdf",
      "contentType": "application/pdf",
      "size": 2048
    },
    "submissionToken": "{{quoteRecapToken}}"
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("resolves to the SAME recap — no duplicate", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().id).to.equal(bru.getVar("createdQuoteRecapId"));
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /quote-recaps (replay) — idempotency check (PAC-39)

  Re-sends the previous request **with the same `submissionToken`** and asserts
  the response carries the same recap id. Run it straight after
  `Create Quote Recap`, which captures both the id and the token.

  - **Module:** `quote_recaps`
  - **Permission:** `quote_recaps:write`

  ## What this proves
  A double-click, a flaky connection, or a client retry produces **one** recap,
  not two. Two duplicate recaps would double-count premium in the Quoted
  scorecard, which is the failure this guards against.

  Three layers make it hold:

  1. **A pre-transaction probe.** The token is looked up before any work, so a
     replay never re-resolves the household or re-verifies storage.
  2. **A unique partial index** on `{ agencyId, submissionToken }` — a partial
     filter, deliberately *not* `sparse`, because a compound sparse index only
     skips a document when *every* indexed field is missing, and `agencyId`
     always has a value. Under `sparse` the second token-less (i.e. migrated)
     recap in an agency would fail with E11000.
  3. **An E11000 catch** on create, which re-reads by token and returns the
     winner — so even two genuinely concurrent requests collapse to one recap.

  The token is namespaced server-side (`WEB|<UPPERCASED>`), so clients never need
  to worry about collisions with other channels.

  A replayed token belonging to **another** producer 404s rather than returning
  their recap id — the ownership clamp runs against the found record too.

  ## Response `201`
  Identical to `Create Quote Recap`, with the same `id`.
}
